<UserControl x:Class="Plith.Views.Widgets.ShelfWidget"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:p="clr-namespace:Plith.Views.Presentation"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             Focusable="False">

    <!-- THE SHELF PAGE, WHICH DRAWS NOTHING, and that is the whole of it.

         This page used to draw the shelf: five tiles, a "+N" chip, an empty state and a line
         saying how to open it. It was the second of two shelves. The shelf itself lives in
         Plith.DropCatcher, because a file can only be dragged out of that process's window, and
         since the handover happens on the page turn the catcher's window covers this page about
         25 ms after it arrives. So everything this page drew was a different-looking imitation of
         the page about to replace it, visible for a moment and gone. Reported three times over,
         finally as "why are there two shelves".

         It still has to EXIST. The pager needs an element for its slot, the frame needs something
         to slide in, and the accent-tinted panel behind it is drawn by the frame rather than by
         this control, so a blank page is a blank page of the notch rather than a hole.

         And it has one thing to say. When the catcher cannot be reached the handover never
         happens, nothing covers this page, and the person is left looking at an empty panel with
         no idea why: OnShelfUnavailable puts the reason here. That sentence is the only thing
         this file draws, and it is the reason this file is not simply deleted. -->
    <!-- IT CLAIMS THE PAGE'S BOX even though it draws nothing in it.
    
         A page that measures zero is not merely invisible, it is dangerous: OsdHost.Reposition
         early-returns when the content measures 0 on either axis, and WidgetFrame sizes its track
         from the pages in it. Every other page here is sized by what it draws, and this one draws
         nothing, so it says its size instead. The numbers are the frame's own, less the inset
         that positions the content inside it, so this page occupies exactly what the other four
         do. -->
    <Grid Margin="{x:Static p:NotchGeometry.PageInsetDip}"
          MinWidth="{x:Static p:NotchGeometry.PageContentWidthDip}"
          MinHeight="{x:Static p:NotchGeometry.PageContentHeightDip}">
        <TextBlock x:Name="Hint"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   MaxWidth="280"
                   FontSize="12"
                   TextAlignment="Center"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource NotchInkMuted}"
                   Visibility="Collapsed" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
